# Shell scripts are executed by bash, on the Linux host and (for the deploy
# path) on Windows under Git Bash. CRLF breaks them: bash reads the \r as part
# of the shebang and of every argument, so `bash script.sh` fails outright and
# `bash -n` is the only way to notice before the failure reaches a deploy.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set in this repository, which means a fresh Windows
# checkout rewrites every tracked text file to CRLF. Pinning eol=lf here is
# what keeps the scripts runnable after any clone.
#
# This is a pre-existing hazard, not a new one: it was live before the
# knowledge-graph removal, and it is invisible until someone clones fresh and
# then runs a deploy script.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for files bash reads as data: compose files, env templates and
# systemd units are all consumed by tooling on Linux, never edited as Windows
# text, and a stray CR in a KEY=value line becomes part of the value.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf

# Deliberately NOT set globally (`* text=auto`): that would renormalise every
# tracked file in the repository and bury this change under an unrelated diff.
# Apply it deliberately, per file type, when a type actually needs it.
